From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] server: Say which protocol error killed a thread.

With 0864's probes in place, every secondary thread this process creates
reaches "thread registered" and then hangs in its first server call. Not
one has ever completed init_thread -- the server's own note says
"init_thread 0024 ok" for the main thread and nothing for any other, in
any run. GTA IV is simply the first program to notice, because the thread
it is waiting on is winmm's devices thread.

klog says what becomes of them:

    wineserver: kill_thread 0028 exit=1 violent=1 from 800bd417f
    wineserver: kill_thread 002c exit=1 violent=1 from 800bd417f

and 800bd417f resolves, through kill_thread's own anchor trick, to
fatal_protocol_error+0xcf. So the server is not failing to answer: it
reads something it cannot parse from the new thread's request pipe,
declares a protocol error and kills the thread.

Which protocol error it is, is the one thing not recorded. This function
prints it with fprintf to stderr, and this module's stderr reaches
nothing on this console -- fatal_error and kill_thread, a few lines
below, already use klog for exactly that reason. Send it there too.

The message distinguishes every candidate: "read:" and "partial read" mean
the pipe gave the wrong bytes, "bad request" means it parsed as nonsense,
"reply write:" means the reply pipe is the broken end. Each one points at
a different half of this port's descriptor handover, where a descriptor is
passed by number rather than duplicated (dup is EPERM here) and the two
ends are shared with the in-process server.
diff --git a/server/request.c b/server/request.c
index e92298cf64..d74ea2d80a 100644
--- a/server/request.c
+++ b/server/request.c
@@ -145,6 +145,32 @@ void fatal_protocol_error( struct thread *thread, const char *err, ... )
     fprintf( stderr, "Protocol error:%04x: ", thread->id );
     vfprintf( stderr, err, args );
     va_end( args );
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+    /* ⚠ The line above reaches nothing. This module's stderr goes nowhere on
+     * this console -- klog does, which is why fatal_error and kill_thread
+     * below already use it. Without this the kill is anonymous: klog shows
+     * "kill_thread 0028 exit=1 violent=1 from fatal_protocol_error+0xcf" and
+     * no reason at all.
+     *
+     * It is the reason that matters here. Every secondary thread this process
+     * creates hangs in its first server call and is then killed from exactly
+     * this function, which is why GTA IV never gets the winmm devices thread
+     * it is waiting on, and why no second thread has ever appeared in a log. */
+    {
+        extern int sceKernelDebugOutText( int channel, const char *text );
+        char line[256];
+        int n;
+
+        n = snprintf( line, sizeof(line), "wineserver: protocol error %04x: ", thread->id );
+        if (n > 0 && n < (int)sizeof(line))
+        {
+            va_start( args, err );
+            vsnprintf( line + n, sizeof(line) - n, err, args );
+            va_end( args );
+            sceKernelDebugOutText( 0, line );
+        }
+    }
+#endif
     thread->exit_code = 1;
     kill_thread( thread, 1 );
 }
